1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,807
APIs rarely crash loudly.

2
00:00:01,857 --> 00:00:05,536
They slow down, return empty data, or quietly fail.

3
00:00:05,586 --> 00:00:11,750
API Monitor catches every one of those moments, and tells you why, in real time.

4
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:19,443
Every service streams its telemetry — status code, latency and records returned — into one endpoint.

5
00:00:19,493 --> 00:00:27,648
Eight deterministic rules check each sample: server errors, auth failures, high latency, empty or missing data, and more.

6
00:00:27,698 --> 00:00:29,471
No AI guessing here.

7
00:00:29,521 --> 00:00:31,750
Detection is plain, testable code.

8
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:34,406
And it never spams you.

9
00:00:34,456 --> 00:00:45,688
Repeated failures are grouped by a fingerprint of the API and its symptoms, so a thousand bad responses become one incident with an occurrence count.

10
00:00:45,738 --> 00:00:49,750
If a warning turns critical, it escalates in place.

11
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:57,341
Open an incident and you get a plain-English summary, a probable cause and a recommended fix, written by AI.

12
00:00:57,391 --> 00:01:00,747
But the AI only explains — it never decides.

13
00:01:00,797 --> 00:01:06,424
Its answer must be valid JSON, name the right API, and use only real numbers.

14
00:01:06,474 --> 00:01:14,750
If a provider fails, it switches from Gemini to OpenAI to Mistral, and if all are down, a built-in template takes over.

15
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:23,805
AI costs money, so it only runs where it pays off: automatically for incidents that send an email.

16
00:01:23,855 --> 00:01:32,461
Everything else gets free, rule-based guidance — plus one click on Generate AI analysis when you want more.

17
00:01:32,511 --> 00:01:33,903
Same incident twice?

18
00:01:33,953 --> 00:01:37,750
The cached answer is reused, at zero cost.

19
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:39,615
Everything is live.

20
00:01:39,665 --> 00:01:46,938
The dashboard updates over server-sent events the moment an incident changes — no refresh button.

21
00:01:46,988 --> 00:01:54,750
And a built-in simulator replays twelve real failure scenarios, so you can break things on purpose.

22
00:01:55,200 --> 00:02:03,221
Critical incidents go straight to your inbox through any SMTP provider — here, Gmail.

23
00:02:03,271 --> 00:02:14,750
Choose recipients in Settings, with no restart, and send a test email that reports exactly what the mail server answered.

24
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:22,500
Services shows each API's traffic, error rate and latency against its thresholds.

25
00:02:22,550 --> 00:02:34,750
And every sign-in, role change and resolution lands in the audit log, behind role-based access, rotating tokens and Argon2-hashed passwords.

26
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:47,750
It works on every screen — tables on desktop, cards on your phone — and every endpoint is documented in OpenAPI with a live Swagger UI.

27
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:58,461
Postgres, Redis, the API, the worker, the simulator and the dashboard — all start with one command, backed by over three hundred automated tests.

28
00:02:58,511 --> 00:02:59,750
That's API Monitor.
